  "hints": [
    "comparing against a full reversed copy works but uses O(n) extra space - can you avoid copying the whole list?",
    "you only need to compare the first half against the reversed second half - how would you find where the second half begins without knowing the length up front?",
    "use fast and slow pointers to locate the middle of the list in one pass",
    "reverse the second half starting from the middle, then walk it alongside the first half comparing values one by one"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "getting the middle index wrong for even-length lists and comparing one extra or one fewer element",
    "reversing the whole list instead of just the second half, which loses the first half's original order",
    "not handling the single-element list case (should return true immediately)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]` is the value of the i-th node in order.\n\n**Determine whether the list's values read the same forwards and backwards, that is, whether they form a palindrome.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "list values are not necessarily unique"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list",
      "an even-length list vs an odd-length list",
      "a list that is not a palindrome only in the very last comparison"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
